WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.330
If you visit California,

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.430
you might be inclined to go to Disneyland,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.030
or jump in your convertible

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.020
and take Highway 1 up the coast,

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.480
or maybe make a pilgrimage to Yosemite.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.850
All very fine choices.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.190
But if you're the kind of person

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.600
who ravels in our planet's extremes,

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.890
then it's Route 395 that you're after.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.010
This one glorious road

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.950
will carry you from Death
Valley's desert floor

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.930
to sky high views of Mount Whitney.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:56.430
You can see the 5,000-year-old
bristle cone pines,

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.310
the oldest living organisms.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.160
Or the bizarre tufa towers of Mono Lake.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.640
And true to California's
boom and bust spirit,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.360
you can saunter around
the ghost town of Bodie.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.120
What a novel experience to
find an empty American city,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.110
where all the businesses are shut down.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.210
To do 395 up right,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.890
it helps to have a guide.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.720
And mine is a man named Jan and his crew.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.790
The pass back here.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:37.970
Haryn.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.220
Let's step. Tidy up

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.170
And Lemmy,

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.740
the hardest partying dog of all time.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:49.280
Jan agreed to show me some
of what 395 has to offer,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.790
but only if we agreed not
to disclose any locations.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.000
My name is Jan Zwierstra.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.980
I'm an industrial designer out of LA,

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.280
and I spend a lot of time camping

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.490
and a lot of time in the Eastern Sierra.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.660
It's a place I love.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.640
And sometimes I'm known as 395 North

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.350
because this, yep!

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:16.470
My Instagram account.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.950
And I don't like to be known as that,

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.700
but I'm trying to use
it to set a good example

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.280
and do more with it
than just social media.

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.070
Where did we end up camping?

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:29.170
We got here late last night.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.980
I wasn't sure where we are.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.560
This is BLM land

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.000
right on the outskirts
of the national forest.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.850
And it's one of my favorite
places in the world.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.100
Basically everything outdoors

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:41.930
wrapped into one.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.970
Yeah.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:44.260
Good thing about California, man.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.140
Oh man,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.390
we have so much national
forest and public land

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.430
that you can literally just cruise out

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.200
and find a place to camp.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.080
It's ours.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:02.450
What I wish it was mine
is Jan's sweet truck.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.170
It's a 2019 Ford ranger,

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.720
has about 200 Watts of solar in it

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.560
and a fridge and has
everything that an RV has

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:13.700
except for a toilet,

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.960
but just on a smaller, simple scale.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.730
You drive us around day-to-day in LA

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:19.560
and then just roll out when you want.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.670
Yeah. It's my daily driver.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.030
Handles great, and then
when I'm done with work,

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.160
I can just bail and go camping and out.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.870
So the camper basically
has only what I need.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.670
Think of it as backpacking with a car.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:34.670
Yeah.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:38.000
The fridge has a couple of compartments.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.030
This is just the kitchen box,

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.580
stove, utensils, and a bunch of stuff.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:44.720
This one has just dry food.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.980
Then when you get inside, there's a bed.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.670
The bed flips up. So you've
got tons of room in here.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:52.600
This awning

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.080
is phenomenal.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.690
If you want to pull that over.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:06.760
That is cool.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.040
Let me just pop that up.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:10.960
Sort of.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.610
So home on wheels.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.040
Yeah. This is my vacation house.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:16.490
Let's do this. So nice man.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.260
Even with his nomad lifestyle,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.990
Jan faces the same big
question as the rest of us.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.680
Do the internet and smartphones
have to ruin everything

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.400
when you find the super special,

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.130
beautiful spots is that
you busted out a map

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.740
and like, look at where
might be cool to go,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:37.680
or is it word of mouth

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.360
and just getting to know
people, the locals and.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.090
If I see a dirt road,
I just want to go down.

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.890
So a lot of this is just aimless rambling.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:49.250
It's a desire to just see something,

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.360
check that out.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.610
For some reason,

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:55.810
it's more fulfilling than
getting a recommendation

00:04:55.810 --> 00:05:00.010
from somebody or there's tons
of apps and websites now,

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:00.900
road trip apps,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.020
camping apps,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.170
they proliferate it.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.850
The knowledge is accessible
on the internet, right?

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:10.270
I used to fight the geo tags,

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.900
and I would get really pissed off

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.570
and be like, why are
you posting this place?

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:15.400
It's going to blow up.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.270
It's going to get trashed.

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:20.160
Now I've realized that
you, you can't fight it.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.570
The internet has just shrunk the world.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.450
So we're not going to stop it.

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.440
We're not going to stop
people from going there.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:31.240
So I'm a lot more focused
in conversation now

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.170
with trying to set a good example.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:36.200
When you go, pick up your trash.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.950
When you're pooping out in the wilderness,

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:41.080
don't leave your toilet
paper on the top, you know.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:44.690
Make sure you follow good
steps for leaving no trace.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.950
As a thank you for this quick guide

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.470
to pooping etiquette,

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.860
we made Jan, Haryn and Lemmy some tacos

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.430
and we all took in the night sky

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.370
and knocked back a couple of drinks.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.050
Take that apocalypse.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.830
The thing about good
times and serenity in 2020

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:16.540
is that they're fleeting at best.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.630
After we split up from Jan
and continued on our way,

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.650
smoke started to envelop us.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:32.480
The plus side was that our RV
no longer smelled like feet.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.250
The downside was breathing.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:37.390
My man David here suggested

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.100
that we try and outflank the smoke

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.860
by cutting through Yosemite.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.170
Without a reservation,

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.010
we had to talk our way in.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.830
Yes. No stopping, no getting out.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.450
Don't go to the valley.

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:48.650
Yeah, no problem.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:50.900
We just, yeah, we just
wanted to get through.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.790
It was a beautiful choice.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.320
Glad we got out of that smoke.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.800
Whoa.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.270
But not quite as
effective as we had hoped.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.620
Fast forward four hours or so,

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.550
and we had managed to trade
the smoky majesty of Yosemite

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.950
for the smoky, um,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.150
Look at it.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:21.820
Strip malls of the Central Valley.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.760
We have made it sort of out of the smoke

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.960
all the way across Yosemite National Park

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.420
and here to the gorgeous
Central Valley of California.

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.590
We're off to see a kind
of surprising tech startup

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.970
that is making some
pretty strange machines

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.360
to serve all the growers
out in this region.

00:08:05.070 --> 00:08:07.890
And where was the, where
here and this near Fresno.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.180
This is California farm country, right?

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:12.530
We're just South of Fresno

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.000
town called Kingsburg.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.710
So we're right in the
Central Valley of California,

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:21.270
very, very large farming Valley here.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:24.590
It's kind of considered the
bread basket of the world.

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.440
The central Valley stretches out

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.090
over about 20,000 square miles.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.290
And the farmers here
produce about one fourth

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.520
of the food that Americans eat,

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.160
including a whole lot of fruits and nuts.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:39.260
Tell me a little bit of
the background of guys.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:41.740
I know some of this is
like a family business

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.410
that the roots of this go back ways.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.080
Tell me kind of like how we got,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.880
so it was essentially
like a startup, I guess.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:49.710
Yeah, definitely.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.730
So the founder of our
company is Dave Crinklaw

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.060
and he started the business back in 1982.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.370
He started with his basically,

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:03.370
with his dad was commercial
spraying of trees and vineyards.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:07.650
Got to the point where our
biggest challenge was labor.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:09.640
Just the shortage of it really.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.420
He was forced into innovating

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:14.360
and he had had this
idea for quite some time

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.780
about doing a driverless sprayer.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.270
And it finally got to
the point where he said,

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:21.110
"You know what?

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:22.880
I either got to get out of this business

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.480
or I'm going to innovate."

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:25.480
Organic or not,

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.110
crops need to be sprayed.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:30.320
And this process has to take
place a few times a year.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.150
This means hiring a lot of
people to work long hours

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.690
under serious pressure.

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.900
And so just like the
tech bros to the North,

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:41.340
GUSS decided to solve all
of its problems with robots.

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.630
On this like labor points,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:45.700
you guys have set this up

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.760
where you can have
somebody you know, track

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.660
and that one person can monitor,

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.000
I think like eight of these.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.220
In order to operate GUSS,

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:55.390
you have one guy or girl
that sits in a pickup

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.970
and they monitor from a laptop computer.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.440
So that person is basically
just watching all the vehicles

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.820
on the screen.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.480
They can monitor up to
eight of them at a time

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.390
while they're out there,
spraying in the orchard.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:09.820
And so that user interface

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.380
really provides them with
all the information they need

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.850
to make sure that the machines are safe

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.980
and that they're doing the correct job.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.660
So it tells them what
speed they're driving,

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.120
the engine RPM,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.270
the amount of gallonage per acre

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:26.060
that they're applying at any given time.

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.820
So if any one of the
machines has an issue,

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.690
it's going to send an
alert to that laptop.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.350
Much like a self-driving car,

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:36.560
these beasts use GPS, LiDAR and cameras

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.930
to see and navigate the world around them.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.380
They also rely on pre-built
maps of the orchards

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:43.600
for extra guidance.

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.030
GUSS builds the machines right here

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.790
and then takes them out for robot training

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:54.800
on this orchard next door.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:56.300
It's here that the company learns

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:57.940
if the machines will behave

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.450
and do as their software
and human masters command.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.460
So this is kind of like the,

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:04.820
this is the test orchard

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.030
where you guys put it through the paces

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.810
and work on their technology?

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:11.960
Yeah. So this is our
test orchard right here.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.070
It's right by our building

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.830
as you can see in the background.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.290
So every new machine,

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:18.350
as it comes off the assembly line,

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.110
it's put through a
commissioning test right here.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:23.360
We test all the sensors on the machine,

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:25.060
autonomous computer,

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:28.230
all the safety systems make
sure it's doing what it's doing

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.380
prior to when it's
delivered to a customer.

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.510
So what's it doing now?

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.600
So right now he's doing a test spray.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.180
So he's just turning the water on

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:41.080
to make sure that the
system comes up to pressure,

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.670
spraying water out of the nozzles,

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.070
just to make sure it's
all functioning properly.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:46.730
Okay.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:49.130
It's got like a crazy
turbine at the back or?

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.380
Yeah. So it's basically just a big fan

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:55.150
and that fan's driven off
of the Cummins diesel motor.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:58.150
And it just basically
creates a big airflow.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.440
GUSS is already selling these vehicles

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.010
to brave modern farmers.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.220
And people of the world over

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:07.620
have taken notice of its machines.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:09.840
And have you just sold
them in the central Valley

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:10.920
or like throughout the US

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:11.840
or even overseas,

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:13.000
or where like where are they going?

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.070
Yeah so,

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:16.670
right now the majority of our machines

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.100
are here in California
in the Central Valley.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:23.330
However, we do have machines
and customers in Florida

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.160
and the Citrus Industry over there

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:26.440
as well as Australia.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:27.270
And I think you started selling them

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:29.900
in like December of 2019, right?

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:30.730
Yeah.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.960
So we started taking
orders early last year

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:34.170
for the sprayers.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:38.340
And the first delivery we
made were in December of 2019.

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:41.260
Okay. And how much do they cost each?

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.120
So our retail price

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.600
on these machines is $285,000.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:46.430
So it's like,

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:47.530
it's not a small investment,

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.460
but I'm sure the regular
sprayers aren't cheap either.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:51.310
Yeah. I mean, we're,

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.100
we're honestly not too far away

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.580
from conventional equipment on our price.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.260
And the main thing is that
the return on investment

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:01.660
is very, very quick due
to all the labor savings

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.440
and the increased efficiency.

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:05.960
I mean these machines, they just,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.670
they just thrive.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.080
But basically what we're
doing besides this machine,

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:11.620
this is our first machine,

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.080
we call it Orchard GUSS,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.910
mainly designed for your nut trees,

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.050
as well as citrus and fruit.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.850
In the future, we're working
on a few other projects.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.740
We're going to do more machines.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.410
One of them is actually
a vineyard sprayer.

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:28.080
So vineyards are much tighter spacing.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.450
So the machine has to
be physically smaller.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.960
And then we've got our eyes

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:33.800
on doing a lot of other innovating.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.020
That right there friends,

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.470
is a Central Valley farmer

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:44.020
putting his whole fruit
basket on the table.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:47.140
Gary's Ted Talk can't be far off.

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:50.710
And with that,

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:52.490
it was time to get back in the RV.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.460
And to confront what happens

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:01.380
when three men are bested
by their own failings

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:04.770
in the forces of entropy.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.810
Next on Hello World,

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:18.820
we cruise into the Mojave Desert

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:20.820
in search of a lunar lander.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:24.120
We have instruments that
we are going to deliver

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:28.080
to the South Pole of the
moon in December, 2022.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.240
Instruments that are trying to
scan the surface of the moon.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.120
And hang with a test pilot

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:36.840
who walked away from this.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.470
So we just finished part
two of a three part series

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.250
of Ashlee and the team Hello world's

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.390
big trip down highway 395.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.530
Now we seem to have gotten
a little farther South.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.340
We're like, kind of in, well,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.560
we're an undisclosed locations

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.460
through sort of the Eastern Sierras

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:02.850
and then a little bit farther
in the Central Valley.

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:05.910
But so first I wanna ask
you how you found Yon.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.410
Like there seems to be
this tension where he like,

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:11.510
he feels like this man with conflicts,

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:13.620
like he wants to put all
this beautiful footage out

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.170
and so wonderful to look at.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:18.240
And yet there seems to be
the sense of tension of like,

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.250
Oh, is he contributing to a kind of way

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:23.850
of consuming the world that
he doesn't really believe in?

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:25.610
Yeah, well that's true.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:27.930
That Yon, he was like
awesome to hang out with.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.430
And his whole crew, they were
really fun to hang out with,

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.230
but he was actually hard to get ahold of.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:34.690
We found him the,

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.930
my awesome producers on the show

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.010
found him and reached out.

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:41.460
And then, you know, part of it was like,

00:15:41.460 --> 00:15:43.700
I think we were like trying to
contact him for like a month

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:44.860
that it was very spotty

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.410
'cause he would just be
like gone for two weeks

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:50.160
and have no connection
and not really get back.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.880
And then I think there
were a couple of emails

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.040
where he's like, I wanna do it

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:55.330
but I kinda don't wanna do it.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:58.240
I think once we hung out,
we got along so well.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.410
And I think he'd watch some of the shows

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:04.010
and he was, he knew we weren't
trying to like, you know,

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:05.780
do some gotcha.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:09.970
We're gonna expose all your
secret spots to everybody

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.170
so it ended up being okay.

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:15.410
What was the pool you were
in, like hot or cold pool?

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:17.060
Yeah, I hope I described as right

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.260
and Yon doesn't get mad at me.

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:23.680
I mean, it was just out
on some, some landlords

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:26.530
they kinda like spike down into the ground

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.400
and create this pool.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:30.310
It wasn't super hot, It wasn't cold either

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:31.370
It was kinda of nice.

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:32.200
It was refreshing.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.000
I remember that day it
was really, really hot

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.900
and we'd been stuck in the RV all day.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:41.030
It was good to get out and
sorta feel clean for once.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:47.550
And BLM land is this really
special type of land that's,

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:49.890
I mean, it's in California and obviously

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.290
across the US as well.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:53.920
But that's, you know, like
BLM happens on BLM land.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:56.820
I think you get a better
sense of how much of a

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:00.730
big part of California is
sort of like uninhabited

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:02.690
run, sort of maintained by the government

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.880
that there's, just go and see.

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:05.830
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:05.830 --> 00:17:07.760
And we get into that a little bit.

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:10.680
Chatting with Yon

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.390
and he was talking about the difference,

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.350
off camera he was talking
about going to Texas

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:16.180
and some other States

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:20.050
and just how much harder it
was to find spots like that.

00:17:20.050 --> 00:17:22.420
And people aren't always as excited

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:26.990
to see you like romping around
in some of these places.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.770
And so, yeah, California's
got a magic like that.

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:33.440
And again, even though I live here,

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.060
it was a reminder that I don't
take advantage of this stuff

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:37.880
as much as I should.

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.910
It was nice to have like
somebody who really knew

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:42.290
what they were doing and to remind you

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.620
that you just need to
think more creatively.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:46.210
Yeah, and then you went to,

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:49.960
another big aspect of
California's vast land

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.750
being rows and rows and
rows and rows of orchards

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:55.750
not fruits which is the
kind of stuff you drive past

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:58.380
when you're going to
70 and stuff like that.

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:04.240
How did you find the these robot spray?

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:05.790
Orchard robot sprayers?

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.210
Yeah, this was a cool one.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:09.780
I mean, this is one where like
somebody reached out to me

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:11.570
either on Twitter or
Instagram and they're like,

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:14.810
"You gotta see this thing
and these, these guys

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.010
down in the Central
Valley, they're selling.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:18.940
Yeah, It's like all
those Silicon Valley guys

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:20.490
talking about their self-driving cars,

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:21.360
they don't even sell it.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:22.810
These guys are like selling these things

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:24.860
that can't even make them fast enough."

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:27.200
And I kind of ignored it
the first couple of times

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:28.730
this guy was persistent.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:29.770
He didn't even work at guys.

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.530
He was like lives out there.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.770
And then finally, you know,
I pulled up the picture

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:36.020
I'm like, Oh yeah, I
gotta go see this thing.

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.470
And then their website, like,
it kind of blew me away too.

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.170
I don't wanna seem like,

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:46.190
I'm trying to like downplay the technology

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:47.420
of the Central Valley and stuff,

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:49.210
but their site was pretty amazing.

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:50.430
And I have these likes, you know,

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:54.930
the super impressive videos
of this stuff in action.

00:18:54.930 --> 00:18:57.110
And so once I saw that,
it actually seemed real.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:00.530
I was like, Oh yeah, I
wanna go, go see these guys.

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:02.250
They were kind of nonchalant about how

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.160
they had like put it together,

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:06.730
which I thought was impressive
and like so different

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:08.160
to the Valley, as you know,

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.440
where like everyone's telling
you how awesome they are

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.100
all the time and these
guys were just like,

00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:16.470
"Yeah, we just picked up some LIDAR.

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.870
And like, we had a couple guys like,

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:20.340
write the software for this

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:22.340
and we got some help from this company."

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:23.390
Yeah, they're like next thing you know

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:25.270
the sprayer is driving itself.

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:26.890
I was like, okay.

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:27.900
How much of a difference does it,

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:30.680
I know you mentioned
that they also use pre,

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.500
sort of pre drawn out maps all through

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:33.880
which I have to imagine
it's pretty helpful.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.120
You're also not dealing
with like cross traffic,

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:38.640
like, I mean yeah.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:41.500
Like the bicycles in the orchard.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:44.550
No, for sure, the
difficulty of like getting

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.030
a self-driving car to work

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:49.900
is much, much harder, and they do, yes.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.700
The pre drawn maps totally helpful.

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.340
I mean, you can get all these
like satellite images now,

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.940
or aerial footage.

00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:59.790
And then with GPS, I mean,

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:03.090
you're telling this thing
exactly where it needs to go.

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:06.640
And then a lot of the,
LIDERS and the cameras

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:08.620
other thing, I think
it mostly just in case

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:11.140
something runs into it or
something like a tree's

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:13.690
fallen down since the pictures were taken

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:16.250
and so a lot of it's for safety.

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:18.130
But I still thought it
was still surprising to me

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:21.350
'cause I done this for a long
time and you just don't have,

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:24.020
I've never really had a
startup from the Central Valley

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:25.890
reach out to me at all, you know?

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:27.150
And then I have been,

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:29.580
I got into like AG-tech for a while

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:30.970
and I noticed that

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:33.420
people were doing cool stuff

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.830
but they did kind of like
tend to keep it to themselves.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:38.800
It was like this farm would make all this

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.010
sort of custom machine

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:41.140
but not really sell it.

00:20:41.140 --> 00:20:43.420
And so that kinda caught my attention

00:20:43.420 --> 00:20:45.100
that these guys had
shipped them all the way

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.740
to places like Australia already.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:49.130
Yeah, that's pretty cool.

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:51.770
Ag tech is, it's pretty wild.

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:55.420
Like I know someone who runs a dairy farm

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:58.380
and they milk the cows using a robot

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:02.110
that uses lasers to locate the utter.

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:04.050
The cow walks in and then
there's like some lasers

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:05.820
and then it just automatically milks it,

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:06.840
It's just incredible.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:08.900
Yeah, the computer vision
stuff's getting weird.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.640
So I know there's other company in Idaho

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.010
and they're usually computer vision.

00:21:12.010 --> 00:21:14.650
So like picking rock is
like a big thing on farms.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.850
I always pictured in my head
that you got out in the field

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.490
and you pull off the rocks
once and that's sort of it,

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:22.800
but they get churned up by
the ground all the time.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:26.500
And so this is like this annual
exercise where like humans

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:29.060
have to go out and scour the field,

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:30.170
they carry all this heavy stuff

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:32.020
and so, you know, this
other company's using

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.880
computer vision and robot
hands to go do that.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:39.450
So I guess, I'm sure over
the next 10, 20 years

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:42.000
is gonna get pretty weird out on the farm.

